      <header class="stem-head">
        <div class="kicker">explaining myself // the long version</div>
        <h2 class="section-title" id="pe-title">policy engineer?</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          People ask me why I put engineer next to policy. Fair question. So
          here is the long version: a stem that grows as you scroll, with
          every part of the answer hanging off it.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="pe-node left">
        <div class="pe-stalk" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="pe-leaf leaf-up" viewBox="0 0 34 18" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 17 C 6 4, 20 0, 33 3 C 26 13, 12 18, 1 17 Z" /></svg>
        </div>
        <article class="pe-chunk">
          <header class="pe-chunk-head"><h3>the short answer</h3><span class="pe-idx mono">01</span></header>
          <p>I design, build, test, and improve policy as a complete working system. Not a document that gets filed away. A live system that people use, one that has to work in the real world.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="pe-node right">
        <div class="pe-stalk" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="pe-leaf leaf-down" viewBox="0 0 34 18" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 17 C 6 4, 20 0, 33 3 C 26 13, 12 18, 1 17 Z" /></svg>
        </div>
        <article class="pe-chunk">
          <header class="pe-chunk-head"><h3>the full stack of policy</h3><span class="pe-idx mono">02</span></header>
          <p>Software has a stack. So does policy, and I work across all of it.</p>
          <div class="pe-rows">
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key">frontend</span><span>the part people see and use: services, forms, messages, offices, digital access</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key">backend</span><span>the base that makes it work: law, budget, data, staff, authority, infrastructure</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key">middle layer</span><span>the connective tissue: process, data flow, decision rights, feedback, operations</span></div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="pe-node left">
        <div class="pe-stalk" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="pe-leaf leaf-up" viewBox="0 0 34 18" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 17 C 6 4, 20 0, 33 3 C 26 13, 12 18, 1 17 Z" /></svg>
        </div>
        <article class="pe-chunk">
          <header class="pe-chunk-head"><h3>how I work</h3><span class="pe-idx mono">03</span></header>
          <p>Technology and computation are in the toolkit, but so are systems thinking and qualitative judgment. Data shows patterns, human insight gives them meaning. I do not force one fixed method onto every problem. I design the response that fits the problem, then innovate, improve, and help good methods spread.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="pe-node right">
        <div class="pe-stalk" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="pe-leaf leaf-down" viewBox="0 0 34 18" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 17 C 6 4, 20 0, 33 3 C 26 13, 12 18, 1 17 Z" /></svg>
        </div>
        <article class="pe-chunk">
          <header class="pe-chunk-head"><h3>from intent to system</h3><span class="pe-idx mono">04</span></header>
          <p>Policy engineering is the practice of turning public intent into a working system. Start with a clear view of the problem: map the system, identify the people affected, study the causes, test possible actions. Build, and connect <a class="pe-link" href="/projects/manifesto-analysis/" title="see the build: manifesto analysis in code">law, service, technology, and operations</a> so it works in practice, not only on paper. Then monitor the results and correct the design.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="pe-node left">
        <div class="pe-stalk" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="pe-leaf leaf-up" viewBox="0 0 34 18" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 17 C 6 4, 20 0, 33 3 C 26 13, 12 18, 1 17 Z" /></svg>
        </div>
        <article class="pe-chunk">
          <header class="pe-chunk-head"><h3>built for wicked problems</h3><span class="pe-idx mono">05</span></header>
          <p>The hardest problems in government are wicked: many causes, many people, shifting over time, no single right answer. A fixed process fails here. So I build <a class="pe-link" href="/projects/pmjay-system-dynamics/" title="see the build: PM-JAY system dynamics">systems that learn</a>. They use evidence. They respond to human need. They stay safe under pressure.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="pe-node right">
        <div class="pe-stalk" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="pe-leaf leaf-down" viewBox="0 0 34 18" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 17 C 6 4, 20 0, 33 3 C 26 13, 12 18, 1 17 Z" /></svg>
        </div>
        <article class="pe-chunk">
          <header class="pe-chunk-head"><h3>what it is not</h3><span class="pe-idx mono">06</span></header>
          <p>A policy engineer is not someone who stamps an existing process onto a new problem. No copying a template and calling it a solution. Policy is not a fixed object. It is a living system that affects real people, and it deserves the same care as anything load-bearing.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="pe-node left">
        <div class="pe-stalk" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="pe-leaf leaf-up" viewBox="0 0 34 18" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 17 C 6 4, 20 0, 33 3 C 26 13, 12 18, 1 17 Z" /></svg>
        </div>
        <article class="pe-chunk">
          <header class="pe-chunk-head"><h3>principles I work from</h3><span class="pe-idx mono">07</span></header>
          <div class="pe-rows">
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key">01</span><span>Start with the problem, never with a preferred tool.</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key">02</span><span>Design for people: easy to use, respectful, fitted to real conditions.</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key">03</span><span>Use evidence and judgment together. Data shows patterns, judgment gives meaning.</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key">04</span><span>Build for change. Update the policy when the evidence changes.</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key">05</span><span>Make the system safe. Test before wide use, look for harm, protect people from failure.</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key">06</span><span>Share what works. Record results, share methods, help the field grow.</span></div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="pe-node right">
        <div class="pe-stalk" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="pe-leaf leaf-down" viewBox="0 0 34 18" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 17 C 6 4, 20 0, 33 3 C 26 13, 12 18, 1 17 Z" /></svg>
        </div>
        <article class="pe-chunk">
          <header class="pe-chunk-head"><h3>engineering, applied</h3><span class="pe-idx mono">08</span></header>
          <p>The vocabulary transfers cleanly, and each term is a working habit:</p>
          <div class="pe-rows">
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key"><a class="pe-link" href="/projects/critical-minerals-reserve/" title="see the build: critical minerals reserve">load-bearing</a></span><span>rules designed to carry public demand, legal pressure, and crisis</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key"><a class="pe-link" href="/projects/combat-drones-sd/" title="see the build: combat drones scenario model">stress testing</a></span><span>what happens to the policy under scarcity, misuse, and shock</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key"><a class="pe-link" href="/projects/pmjay-system-dynamics/" title="see the build: PM-JAY system dynamics">feedback loops</a></span><span>data, complaints, and measures that keep the system honest</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key"><a class="pe-link" href="/projects/pm-speeches-dashboard/" title="see the build: PM speeches dashboard">friction reduction</a></span><span>removing delay, confusion, and waste from public processes</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key"><a class="pe-link" href="/projects/spatial-crosswalk/" title="see the build: the district spatial crosswalk">refactoring</a></span><span>simplifying law or process without losing the original intent</span></div>
            <div class="pe-row"><span class="pe-key"><a class="pe-link" href="/projects/green-bond-npv/" title="see the build: green bond NPV model">catalysts</a></span><span>incentives and information that let a small action produce a large effect</span></div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="pe-node left">
        <div class="pe-stalk" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="pe-leaf leaf-up" viewBox="0 0 34 18" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 17 C 6 4, 20 0, 33 3 C 26 13, 12 18, 1 17 Z" /></svg>
        </div>
        <article class="pe-chunk finale">
          <header class="pe-chunk-head"><h3>why it matters</h3><span class="pe-idx mono">09</span></header>
          <p>Policy affects real people. A bad process wastes time, causes harm, and erodes trust. A good process gives access, solves problems, and restores confidence. Done with care, policy engineering makes public systems safer, simpler, and more human. In one sentence:</p>
          <p class="pe-punch">A policy engineer builds policy systems that are fit for the problem, safe for people, and ready to improve.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="stem-tail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="stem-bloom" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 96 96" fill="none">
          <defs>
            <radialGradient id="bloom-glow">
              <stop offset="0" stop-color="#4ade80" stop-opacity="0.22" />
              <stop offset="1" stop-color="#4ade80" stop-opacity="0" />
